<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Houmuwu ding</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Simuwu ding</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Houmuwu ding is a rectangular bronze cauldron of the late Shang dynasty, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> in 1939. Weighing about 830 kilograms, it is the heaviest bronze vessel known from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its inscription dedicates it to a royal mother named Wu, probably a consort of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-ding">Wu Ding</a>, and it is now in the National Museum of China in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/houmuwu-ding.jpg" alt="Houmuwu ding" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mlogic · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HouMuWuDingFullView.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
